  "report": "## Report for m-pv4ger\n\nI stopped after 10 of the 16 trials. The last three (t08, t09, t10) did not improve on the best score, and the rules say to stop at that point. The metric is validation accuracy on 999 images, so one image is about 0.1 point.\n\n**Dataset.** It has 11.8k training images of 320\u00d7320 RGB, with two classes. The classes are imbalanced: about 89% negative in my 200-image sample.\n\n**Best configuration.** `convnext_tiny`, `img_size` 224, `lr` 1e-4, 10 epochs, `max_train` 2000. It reached a validation score of **0.979** (t04).\n\n| Trial | Setup | Val score |\n|---|---|---|\n| t01 | resnet18, 128px, 2k images | 0.946 |\n| t02 | convnext_tiny, 128px, 2k | 0.970 |\n| t03 | resnet18, 224px, 2k | 0.941 |\n| t04 | **convnext_tiny, 224px, 2k** | **0.979** |\n| t05 | convnext_tiny, 128px, full data | 0.974 |\n| t06 | convnext_tiny, 224px, full data | 0.979 |\n| t07 | resnet50, 224px, 2k | 0.945 |\n| t08 | convnext_tiny, 224px, 2k, lr 5e-5 | 0.975 |\n| t09 | convnext_tiny, 288px, 2k | 0.969 |\n| t10 | convnext_tiny, 224px, 2k, seed 1, weight decay 0.05 | 0.973 |\n\n**What helped**\n- **Backbone:** ConvNeXt-tiny beat the ResNets by about 2.5 to 3 points at the same size and data.\n- **Resolution:** Going from 128 to 224 gave about 1 point for ConvNeXt, though that gap is close to noise.\n\n**What did not help**\n- **Full training set:** Using all 11.8k images gave no gain at 224 (0.979 either way) and cost about 5 times more time. It gave +0.4 at 128, which is within noise.\n- **Larger images:** 288px was worse (0.969). 320px was refused for memory.\n- **Learning rate:** Lowering it to 5e-5 did not help (0.975). The change was small, as expected.\n- **ResNets:** ResNet-50 at 224 was no better than ResNet-18 at 128, and ResNet-18 did not improve with resolution.\n\n**Confirmation run.** The seed check (t10) scored 0.973, which is 0.6 points below the best. I also changed weight decay to 0.05 in that run, which breaks the one-change rule, so it only gives a rough noise estimate. Together with the noisy validation curves (they swing 1 to 2 points between epochs), this means the real gap between ConvNeXt configurations at 128 to 224px is probably under 1 point. The 0.979 is likely a slightly lucky peak, and I would expect about 0.97 to 0.98 in practice.\n\n**What I would try next**\n- A clean second-seed repeat of the best config.\n- Fewer epochs (5 to 6), since the best epoch is usually 3 to 6 and later epochs overfit.\n- Ensembling or averaging checkpoints to reduce the epoch-to-epoch noise.\n- A larger ConvNeXt (small) at 224px.\n",
